The way I see it, there are two kinds of learning necessary for NetHack.
The fist is to learn _what_ is dangerous and can get you killed, i.e. which
monsters are considerably more dangerous than others of the same level
(those are monsters stronger than their level indicates, like ants or
mumakil, and those with nasty special abilities, like floating eyes or 
mind flayers), and which actions or strategies turn out to behave not like
you expected. The way to learn these is by dying a lot.

The next step is to learn what tactics can be applied to circumvent these
dangers. The best way to do that (apart from listening to the advise of
others) is to experiment in wizard mode.

The first stage can be sped up by the use of explore mode and saying "n" at
the "die?" prompt or by savescumming. Both are not necessarily a good idea,
because it is tempting to dismiss some deaths as "simple stupidity, I'll
just not do that again" and then forget all about it. But when done right
and cautiously, it can be beneficial.

As soon as you're past the first stage and can judge a given situation as 
dangerous before it's too late, you can always stop and look at your
options. If you're not sure what will help you, the easiest way is to
recreate the situation in wizard mode and do some tests.

My point earlier in the thread was that savescumming in the first stage is
unnecessarily complicated compared to explore mode.

